<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#haveli">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haveli</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A haveli is a traditional townhouse or mansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, built around one or more inner courtyards. Wealthy merchant families, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, decorated their havelis with carved stone or wooden facades, balconies and wall paintings. Famous examples survive in Jaisalmer, the Shekhawati region and the old cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haveli.jpg" alt="Haveli" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Taveneaux · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patwon_ki_haveli_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
